Added the claims contract: what this server tells a relying party about a user, and on whose authority. Claims are read from **Plone user properties**, never from a `Profile` — which keeps the `[server]` layer independent of the `[profile]` layer as the import-linter contract requires, and is also simply correct, because the profile layer serves its fields *as* a property sheet through its `IPropertiesPlugin`. A federation therefore looks like two mappings (provider to Profile, Profile to the downstream site's property sheet) and is one: configure the first hop and the second follows, on a site with the profile layer or without it. Scopes release registered OIDC claims only — `profile` gives `name`, `preferred_username` and `website`; `email` gives `email` and `email_verified`; `address` maps Plone's single-line `location` to the `formatted` member; `sub` is never scope-gated. `preferred_username` is the login name rather than the userid, since a userid here is 32 hex characters and means nothing to a person. A claim with no value is omitted rather than sent blank, so a relying party can tell "we do not know" from "it is blank". `email_verified` is the one to read twice: it is true only when *this site* verified the address with a magic link, never because an upstream provider asserted it. The core layer already refuses a provider's word when consuming identities, and an authorization server that passed that word along as its own would export the problem to every relying party downstream. Plone's `description` and any site-added Profile field are not emitted at all: there is no registered claim for them, and a private one would carry something no other implementation can read. Documented in `docs/claims.md`.
